- Edge case: person takes brick off spawn, but we have a stall expected when they press T to validate. Then, they can't put their brick back on spawn since another brick has replaced

- MVP for instruction monitor done. See `instr-monitor.md` for more edge cases to test. 

- `bobBaseHeight` in InstructionBrick.cs doesn't seem to do anything.

- `unity/Assets/CPUWrapper/CPU.cs` and `bridge/CPU.cs` are out of sync. The one under Unity/Assets has more commits.

- KNOWN NATIVE BUG (worked around, not fixed): calling cpu.Dispose() (Assets/CPUWrapper/CPU.cs)
  -> cleanup_design_wrapper() -> design->final() (verilator/bridge.cpp) hangs the Unity Editor
  completely (freeze, zero console output, must force-quit). Reproduced with a full computer
  restart, so it's not an orphaned-process/locked-DLL issue — it's a real bug in the native
  Verilator teardown path. Workaround in place: CPUController.InitCPU() never calls Dispose(),
  just drops the old `cpu` reference and lets a new CPU(instructions) take over (same pattern
  OnDestroy() already used via GC.SuppressFinalize without Dispose()). This leaks the old
  native Verilated model on every level reload — acceptable for now given how few reloads
  happen per session, but should be fixed properly in bridge.cpp eventually (investigate
  design->final() — possibly hangs on trace/waveform flush or re-entrant finalize).

- SUSPECTED RTL BUG: decode-stage addr_rs1/addr_rs2 appear swapped/wrong for I-type
  instructions, causing the register file's rs1 read port to return 0 instead of the
  actual source register's value (so ALU/writeback data comes out as 0 for e.g.
  `addi x1, x2, 1`, instead of x2's value + 1).
  Repro: load level_01.json (single instruction `addi x1, x2, 1`, hex 0x00110093,
  expected decode: rd=1, rs1=2, no rs2). Pick up the brick at StartPlatform, carry it
  through Fetch -> Decode -> Execute -> Memory -> Writeback, pressing T at each station.
  Debug logging added at Assets/UI/TickButtonHandler.cs:35-40 (the `[ALU]` log line)
  prints a_sel/b_sel/alu_sel/addr_rs1/addr_rs2/addr_rd/data_rs1/data_rs2/imm/aluOut/
  regs[1]/regs[2] on every tick press — enable Play Mode and watch the Console.
  Observed at the Execute-stage tick (pc=0x01000010): addr_rs1=1, addr_rs2=2,
  data_rs1=0, data_rs2=17825792 (=regs[2], correct stack-pointer init value).
  Expected: addr_rs1=2 (x2, the actual source register), data_rs1=17825792.
  Instead addr_rs1 shows 1 (x1, the *destination* register), and addr_rs2 shows what
  should have been rs1's address. regs[2] itself is confirmed correct in the register
  file (logged directly), so the register file's contents are fine — the bug is in
  what address gets fed to the rs1/rs2 read ports at decode time, upstream of the
  register file and ALU. Suspect decoder.v / pd.v's addr_rs1_dx_r / addr_rs2_dx_r
  wiring for I-type instructions (immediate/rs2-absent case). Not yet fixed —
  investigate verilog/design/code/decoder.v and pd.v next.